  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Endometriosis with a left ovarian endometrioma (N80.102)</content>. Good response to continuous suppression: worst pain 4 of 10 from 8, no missed work, light spotting only, naproxen rarely needed. Blood pressure 114/72; no migraine with aura. Hemoglobin 12.8 g/dL. No reason for surgery. [S]<list><item>Continue the continuous combined pill.</item><item>Repeat transvaginal ultrasound before the next visit.</item><item>Influenza vaccine given today; she declined the COVID-19 vaccine for now.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Pelvic floor muscle dysfunction with myofascial pain (M62.89)</content>. The deep pain with intercourse that persists despite suppression comes largely from tight, tender pelvic floor muscles, a common companion of endometriosis pain that responds to physical therapy rather than to more hormones or surgery. [S]<list><item>Refer to pelvic floor physical therapy weekly for six to eight weeks.</item><item>A water-based lubricant and unhurried intercourse; stop if painful.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute cystitis (N30.00)</content>. Resolved; culture-sensitive organism treated. [S]<list><item>No further action.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Major depressive disorder, single episode, moderate (F32.1)</content>. PHQ-9 6 from 12, a 50% response, with cognitive behavioral therapy and better pain control; GAD-7 5. No antidepressant is needed. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide or self-harm; the safety plan is current. [S]<list><item>Continue therapy every two weeks.</item><item>PHQ-9 and GAD-7 at the next visit.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Pelvic pain score (0 to 10), pain with intercourse and bowel movements, and missed working days</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Worst pain 4 of 10, from 8; deep pain with intercourse; no bowel pain; no missed work for two months</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Bleeding pattern on continuous suppression</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No periods; light spotting a day or two a month</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Combined pill: adherence, side effects, and reasons to stop estrogen (migraine with aura, high blood pressure, clot symptoms, smoking)</td><td>Every visit, with blood pressure</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Taken daily; blood pressure 114/72; no migraine with aura or clot symptoms</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Naproxen: days used each month, and stomach or kidney symptoms</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two or three days a month; no stomach pain</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>No opioid, benzodiazepine, or sleep-medicine accumulation; prescription monitoring program</td><td>At the start, then every visit while pain is active</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No opioid, benzodiazepine or sleep medicine; prescription monitoring program checked today: no new prescriptions</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hemoglobin</td><td>At baseline with heavy bleeding; again on treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>12.8 g/dL, from 12.2</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Transvaginal ultrasound: endometrioma size and deep disease</td><td>At diagnosis; repeat at about four months on medical treatment, then yearly or if symptoms change</td><td>Due</td><td>Repeat ultrasound booked before the next visit</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Pelvic examination</td><td>At diagnosis, and when pain persists or changes</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Tender levator ani muscles reproduce her pain; uterosacral tenderness less</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Surgical evaluation (laparoscopic excision)</td><td>If pain does not respond to medical treatment, the cyst grows, or imaging is atypical</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Pain responding to medical treatment</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Pelvic floor physical therapy</td><td>When pelvic floor muscle pain is found</td><td>Referred</td><td>Weekly for six to eight weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Pregnancy intention and fertility plan</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Still planning a pregnancy in about a year; folic acid daily</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression measure (PHQ-9)</td><td>Monthly during acute treatment, every 2 to 4 weeks in continuation, then periodically</td><td>Deferred</td><td>PHQ-9 6 today, from 12 at intake and 8 at nine weeks. The PHQ-9 due about four weeks into treatment was not done: the only visit then was the sick visit for cystitis, and the measure was left for the preventive visit at nine weeks. No harm resulted: scores fell at each check and safety was reviewed at the sick visit. Corrective action: the behavioral health clinician records the PHQ-9 at each session and sends it to the chart</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Anxiety measure (GAD-7)</td><td>At diagnosis and with each depression measure</td><td>Completed today</td><td>GAD-7 5, from 8</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide-risk review, safety plan, lethal means, and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of suicide or self-harm; safety plan current</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Cognitive behavioral therapy engagement</td><td>Weekly for the first month, then every two weeks</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Eight sessions</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Sleep without sedatives</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seven hours without any sleep aid</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Worst pelvic pain 3 of 10 or less, and no missed working days for pain, by four months. In progress; target 2026-10-26 [S]</item><item>No opioid, benzodiazepine or sleep-medicine prescription, and naproxen on 10 days a month or fewer. In progress; target 2026-10-26 [S]</item><item>PHQ-9 score below 5 by four months. In progress; target 2026-10-26 [S]</item><item>A written plan for when and how to try for a pregnancy, made with her before the four-month visit. In progress; target 2026-10-26 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Immunizations given today: Influenza vaccine, 2026-2027 season [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Guideline basis: Practice Bulletin No. 114: Management of Endometriosis (2010); ESHRE guideline: endometriosis (2022); Chronic Pelvic Pain: ACOG Practice Bulletin, Number 218 (2020); CDC Clinical Practice Guideline for Prescribing Opioids for Pain, United States, 2022 (2022); U.S. Medical Eligibility Criteria for Contraceptive Use, 2024 (2024); U.S. Selected Practice Recommendations for Contraceptive Use, 2024 (2024); Anxiety Disorders in Adults: Screening (2023); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026); Adult Immunization Schedule by Medical Condition and Other Indication (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Pelvic floor physical therapy weekly. Transvaginal ultrasound in about five weeks, then a visit to review it, with the pain score, PHQ-9 and GAD-7. Next scheduled visit 2026-10-26: four-month review: pelvic floor physical therapy, follow-up ultrasound, mood, and pregnancy plans. [S]</paragraph></text>

